{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>You have seen, etc.</b> One must know the intention of El Elyon in that He was precise to say <b>which I did to Egypt</b>, for it would have been more fitting for Him to say ״who brought you out of the land of Egypt,״ for that is the essence of the matter to obligate them to submit and to embrace His words for all that HaShem bestowed upon them. And if it were to mention His wonders, they are already implied in His saying ״who brought [you] out of the land of Egypt,״ and it is known that He brought them out only by means of the great plagues." "<b>And behold,</b> according to what we have explained, HaShem intends two things, which are the aspect of love and the aspect of fear. Therefore HaShem inscribed both of them in this utterance. The fear in His saying <b>which I did to Egypt</b> — meaning, which I inflicted with chastisements of vengeance upon those who refuse to do My bidding: how many countless evil plagues upon those who refused to do My commandment to send you forth, and this matter was for you a sign regarding one who transgresses My commandment, that I will take vengeance upon him with chastisements of vengeance and many troubles, as you have seen — take admonition. And HaShem intended to allude to them that they are in the very same category as those Egyptians if they refuse to heed the Torah and the commandments, and this is the aspect of fear — fear of punishment. Also, in what is heard from the utterance <b>which I did</b> there is fear of exaltedness, in all the wonders and signs and miracles that He did to Egypt. Moreover, in this He also showed the aspect of love and affection, that He did all this for our sake — He convulsed all of creation and destroyed one nation for our sake. From this side a person ignites with love for the Creator who showered His kindness upon them, and behold, this is the aspect of love for HaShem our Elohim. And these are the two things alluded to in His saying <b>you shall say to the house of Jacob</b> — that fear alone will not suffice and love is also needed, <b>and you shall tell the children of Israel</b> — that the aspect of love alone will not suffice and fear is also needed, and the reason is as we have written." "<b>And I bore you.</b> Behold, the Master elaborated upon the aspect of love, for it is the primary element in the commandment, to yield a double reward, and great exertion is required to attain it. Therefore He said: See My affection for you, like a father's compassion for beloved children — that even from the dust of your feet I had pity and bore you on eagles' wings — meaning, clouds of glory were spread beneath them. And so it says (Deuteronomy 8:4): ״וְרַגְלְךָ לֹא בָצֵקָה.״ Further, the Almighty has more words to arouse love, unlike fear, which enters a person's heart easily; therefore it sufficed for Him in His allusion, in the utterance ״which I did to Egypt,״ as I have explained." "<b>And I brought you to Me.</b> It intends that their drawing near to HaShem was not preceded in the manner of all who draw near to the dwelling of HaShem — that He would first arouse His affection — for it is known that the God of the universe does not initiate drawing near until the one drawing near first arouses love. And so it is from moral instruction, that ordinary and lowly people are the ones who strive to draw near to those greater and more honored than themselves, and all the more so with a ruling servant. Yet even so, HaShem said that it was not so — rather, even though they were a nation from among a nation, naked and bare, without any arousal at all, nevertheless I brought you to Me, and you did not draw near to Me." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to say that after He brought them out of Egypt, they would retreat from following the Shekhinah, as we find of them that they defied the words of El at the Sea of Reeds, in the manna, in the quail, and the like. Yet even so, HaShem would draw them to Him at every time they retreated backward, bringing them and drawing them near to Him, blessed be He." "<b>Further</b>, it intends along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Pesach Haggadah): ״Had the Holy One, blessed be He, not taken our fathers out of Egypt״ etc. — and the reason is so that they would not become sunk in the aspect of evil, in the impurity of Egypt, and so that there not be, God forbid, any further rising for the enemies of Israel. Therefore He hastened to bring them out so that they would stand in a holy place. And this is what He said: <b>וָאָבִא אֶתְכֶם אֵלָי</b> (and I brought you to Myself) — the word אֵלָי is precise to negate their immersion in the portion of evil, may the Merciful One save us. And understand this." "<b>Further</b>, it intends to negate the argument of the objector, for do we not find regarding Him (in the Book of Blessing [Sifrei on Deuteronomy]) that He went to Seir and Paran for Ishmael and Esau and invited them to receive the Torah, and they did not accept it — for the reason that they stated? If so, what is this distinction that He raises over the people of Israel? Therefore He said <b>וָאָבִא</b> etc. — meaning, along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Yalkut HaRe'uveni, beginning of parashat Balak), on the verse (Numbers 23:4): ״וַיִּקָּר אֱלֹהִים אֶל בִּלְעָם״ etc. — a parable of a king whose beloved comes to speak with him, etc. — until here. Meaning: for it is you whom I brought to Myself — unlike them, to whom I sent to their place, to Seir and to Paran; and that was nothing but to reject them for the future and to hold them liable in judgment. But as for you, My intent and My desire is to bring you near to Me, and the deed of Israel is not to be compared to the deed of Edom and Ishmael."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :register {:flags {2 ["the Almighty"], 3 ["God"], 5 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:30:16.610525346Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}